> on Tru64 Unix.  That's too bad, too, because the lack of a working as and ld
> means it's impossible to have a cross development environment target of osf.

Not quite true. I have ported KDE 1.1.2 to Tru64. The only way to do it
was to install the GNU assembler along with gcc-2.95 and it works perfectly. 
There's only one linker error showing up when linking certain C++ programs, 
but it's not serious. All this is on Tru64 4.0F !!

You can get the pre-built development environment from:

ftp://finwds01.tu-graz.ac.at/pub/kde/tru64_4.0x/devel/gcc2.95.1.tru64_4.0f.tar.gz

However: I was unable to install gcc 2.95.2 on Tru64 5.0. I heared that
the situation will improve for the upcoming gcc 2.95.3 so I'm waiting
for this version.

BTW: The reason why I needed gnu-as was that the KDE sources tend to
have really long identifiers which the standard tru64 assembler could not
digest.

And another thing: gcc 2.95.1 *can* be build under Tru64 5.0. There's
a patch for the "configure.*" files on the gnu web site.

