I think it is preferable to use /dev/MAKEDEV tzn where n is the SCSI ID
(shows
up at boot time or use uerf -c oper to check startup log.

>
> However, I cannot access the drive.  mt -f /dev/rmt0h status
> reports "No such device or address".  The scu command show edt
> reports:
>
> CAM Equipment Device Table (EDT) Information:
>
>     Device: CD-ROM XM-6302B Bus: 1, Target: 0, Lun: 0, Type: Read-Only
>                Direct Access
>     Device: BB00921B91 Bus: 2, Target: 0, Lun: 0, Type: Direct Access
>
> What do I need to do to get Unix to recognize the tape drive?
>
> ...

Hi.

You need to update the system configuration and re-compile the kernel.

1. Reboot using generic UX kernel (filename genvmunix)
2. run command sizer -n filename to create a configuration file in
/tmp/filename
3. Edit your system configuration name in /usr/sys/conf/HOSTNAME to
include
   the new SCSI adapter
... something like this ...
device disk     fd0        at fdi0      drive     0
bus             ata0       at pci0      slot  107
controller      scsi0      at ata0      slot  0
bus             ata1       at pci0      slot  207
controller      scsi1      at ata1      slot  0
bus             isp0       at pci0      slot  11 vector    ispintr
controller      scsi2      at isp0      slot  0
...


4. re-compile the kernel with doconfig -c HOSTNAME
5. move the new vmunix kernel to / (make a backup copy of the previous
kernel!)
6. Reboot. The system should create the tape device entries. Make sure you
have
    removed all /dev/*mt0* first otherwise the tape will use device number
1 instead of 0.

I hope I haven't forgotten anything. Good luck,

Pedro..




