Hey group! As you may be aware, the MPAA has filed a lawsuit regarding
the DeCSS program under the Digital Millinium Copyright Act (DMCA).
This is a threat to Linux users world wide!

At 5:40 pm on Thursday, the Motion Picture Association of America was
granted a preliminary injunction prohibiting certain defendants from
distributing the the DeCSS source code. A representative from our
website, The Ultimate DeCSS Resource Site
(http://www.pzcommunications.com/decss/main.htm)was present to hear all
of the arguments presented. As a result of the judge's decision, the
defendants were forced to remove all access to the DeCSS source code
and related information.


Those who are concerned about the freedoms at stake here should also be
concerned about the manner in which this case was brought. The
complaint was filed at the last possible minute on a Friday before a
three day holiday weekend. This was clearly a calculated design to
insure that the defendants had to scramble to find attorneys to then be
able to digest and oppose the hundreds of pages of documents presented
to them. This was also done will full knowledge by the plaintiffs'
attorneys that the EFF legal defense team was busy preparing for the
Tuesday DVD CCA hearing in California. This lack of time made it
virtually impossible for the defendants to assemble any evidence in
time for the hearing while the plaintiffs' attorney had the luxury of
several weeks (if not months) to prepare for this case.

What has occured is a complete miscarriage of justice and something
which should infuriate everyone. The defendants, and presumably
everyone posting DeCSS, will be prohibited from engaging in the very
basic exercise of free speech. Moreover, if this probibition is allowed
to continue and goes unchallenged it will dramatically change the
manner in which the Internet is used and certainly put a dramatic end
to Linux video. The MPAA has convinced the judge that figuring out how
technology works and explaining it to people are illegal acts.
Additionally, they have managed to convince the judge that DeCSS is
about nothing more than piracy of DVDs. This is not the case. DeCSS
research and use is about control. Nothing more than the simple ability
to play DVDs that you lawfully purchased on the machine or platform of
your choice.


