In article <3891D6F5.DF6AF943@lamel.bo.cnr.it>, Eros Albertazzi <eros@lamel.bo.cnr.it> writes:

>I am trying to move an external Seagate barracuda 18LP disk drive, presently
>working attached to a SGI machine, to an Alphastation 500...... if possible...
>
>>>>show dev.... identify it as DKA400....RRD45

This is suspicious.  The RRD45 is the name of a CDROM device.  The Seagate
would be recognized by whatever it puts in its Inquiry data.  This suggests
that you've put the Seagate at the same SCSI address as the CDROM.  If this
is the case, neither will work and it is dicey whether the bus will work.

>How can I put a label on it? Or where can I find an appropriate entry for
>/etc/disktab?

Once you give the disk on its own SCSI target ID and get the console
and operating system to see it, you should simply be able to use the
disklabel command to label it.  If the disk is well behaved, disklabel
will get the geometry information it needs from the driver, which gets
it from the drive.  You won't need a disktab entry (recent versions
don't use disktab anyway).

Adding the disk to an existing bus won't require a kernel rebuild.

