> OK Looking at the file this seems to be the whole charter for the group,
> the file mentioned also seems to imply that lots of sites think this group
> is bogus and should have been rmgrouped anyway, which troubles me somewhat.

Short answer: the rmgroups are entirely bogus.

Long answer: various net.vandals that dislike the Big-8 newsgroup
creation process sometimes try to disrupt it by forging rmgroups of
legitimate groups and newgroups of completely bogus groups.  The
usenet/control files at isc record all control messages for a group,
legitimate or bogus, but there are a variety of signs that make it
obvious which ones are legitimate.  The simplest is that recent
legitimate control messages have a Path of news.isc.org!bounce-back;
the most reliable is to check the X-PGP-Sig, which well administered
news servers will do before honoring the control messages.  The
newgroups scattered in with the rmgroups are automated "boosters" sent
out to counter the bogus rmgroups.  You should find similar activity
in the control record for just about any group you care to try.

There was no vote to rmgroup the group--it's complete fiction.  As far
as I know, no legitimate sites think comp.unix.osf.osf1 is bogus; just
a few kooks and malcontents who think the entire Big-8 is bogus.

As for the RFD, I think it's a good idea.  I'd like to see the removal
of comp.unix.osf.misc added to proposal, with some explanation to the
effect that Tru64 is pretty much the only surviving widely deployed
osf1 derived operating system.
