Hi, Craig -

I've been doing a bit of hunting around for Tru64v5.0-
compatible 3rd-party peripherals myself!

There is a *touch* - but only that - of guidance in
some of the .PDF/.PS on 

    http://www.unix.digital.com/unix/techncl.htm

...but many references to peripherals, alas, are only
for the DECpaq items.

There is a lot of unfiltered rumor & legend out there.
I'll summarize what's been reported to me by RKIs;  I
have not yet booted up my 533MHz 164LX Alpha (gotta get 
RAM, KB, etc. and unfortunately am very busy right now).

[My thanks to Tom Leitner, Patrick Ash, Joe Foehrkolb,
 Steven Eastman, for their info & feedback... ]

General consensus & rumor-mongering regarding non-DEC
peripherals applicable, at least, to my setup (Tru64v5.0 
w/ PC164LX board with new SRM BIOS flashed in):

- Later Toshiba IDE CD-ROMs (5000? 6000? series IIRC)
  should be OK for boot and operation.  Methinks other 
  later/faster CD-ROMs that support the 'El Torito' 
  boot specification and that have some sort of (can't 
  remember exactly which) size parameter that is 512 
  bytes instead of 256 bytes would also work - drives 
  that don't have this 512 value are reported to abort 
  mid-install.

- IDE HDDs don't work.  Guess they figured everyone 
  went SCSI. 

- SCSI controllers:  
    NCR 810-based PCI controllers supposed to be OK.  
    QLogic QLA1040 
    Symbios 53C875 (chip # or card model #?) have been
      touted about as well.
    Prob others... 
    [Apparently Adaptec PCI cards DON'T work!!!]

- Network card: 
    Supposedly any 'Tulip'-type card including 
      DEC/Intel cards w/the DEC 21xxx chip on it.
    Intel EtherExpress PCI w/82558B or later chip;
    3Com EtherLink III - but rumor text has it ISA
      PCMCIA only (PCMCIA on an Alpha?  Methinks
      this is a typo and they mean PCI) - not sure,
      really, which subvariants of this card are 
      go vs. no-go....

- Video:
     Cheapest: S3 Trio 64 PCI;  
       off-brand generic S3 cards appear to work
       except possibly for Diamond Stealth???
     ATI Mach 64 PCI;
     ELSA Gloria PCI variants ($$$!)

- Sound:
     If your Alpha board does not have a custom DEC
     sound chip, you're gonna have to find an ISA
     Microsoft Windows Sound System card.  This card
     is *not* a SoundBlaster and uses an Analog Devices
     Win Sound Sys. chip on it. They are fairly common 
     on EBay.  Believe DEC Multimedia extensions need to
     be loaded; perhaps if a Win Sound Sys card is auto-
     detected, some audio drivers will be installed.

- Keyboard & mouse:  
     I'd keep things real 'generic' here.  Use a std.
     101-key KB w/PS2 connector, and a 'generic' 2-
     button mouse w/PS2 connector.  I'd avoid mice w/
     extra wheels/buttons/wingdings.

- Modems:
     Stay away from PCI/PlugNPray modems, and prob.
     it's a good idea to stay away from internal
     modems in general as well.


Anyway, more info/feedback/correction(s) is/are welcome...


Regards,
Bill

