Hi,

We noticed those warning messages the first time in 4.0d. There it was
obvious. Now in 4.0e it is not obvious anymore. What we do frequently is
following:

We have a large buffer.
We define a structure. The structure is filled with some data and an
extended byte string.
We put the next structure exactly behind the last byte of the byte
string. So this structure might
or might not be aligned anymore. No problem. For that we use memcpy() to
fill in the data.

Now on 4.0e the memcpy(&structure->lrc, &lrc, 1) generates an unaligned
access. Sure that the pointer to structure->lrc is unaligned but for
that we use memcpy()! We solved the unaligned access warning from the
kernel by writing our own Smemcpy() which the copy the source byte by
byte in a loop.

For me it looks like if the compiler though away the memcpy and make it
diffrently. All the castings we tried didn't work out.

Is there an official way how to handle this or a statement why this was
changed in kernel?

Regards,

    Aurel Balmosan




