If the printer has a network interface you can probably use tcp/ip
printing, provided in V4.0a, eliminating the need for two queues.
A tcp/ip print queue looks similar to a parallel port queue with the
exception of LP. For example:

        :lp=@printer.name.com/port_9100:\

Port_9100 is defined in the /etc/services file. ie:

        port_9100       9100/tcp

FYI, 9100 is the port/socket number used for HP and Lexmark printers.

Using this setup you have a direct network connection to the printer with
full print filter functionality. Printcap example:

lp|lp0|0:\
        :ct=tcp:\
        :af=/usr/adm/lpacct_lp0:\
        :if=/usr/lbin/pcfof +Cgeneric_text.pcf:\
        :lf=/usr/adm/lperr_lp0:\
        :lp=@my_printer/port_9100:\
        :mx#0:\
        :of=/usr/lbin/pcfof +Cgeneric_text.pcf:\
        :pl#66:\
        :pw#80:\
        :sd=/usr/spool/print/lp0:\
        :xf=/usr/lbin/xf:



---------------------
