Aurel Balmosan wrote:

>  Now on 4.0e the memcpy(&structure->lrc, &lrc, 1) generates an unaligned
> access. Sure that the pointer to structure->lrc is unaligned but for
> that we use memcpy()! We solved the unaligned access warning from the
> kernel by writing our own Smemcpy() which the copy the source byte by
> byte in a loop.
>

 The V4.0e compiler is generating an inline code sequence to do the
memcpy().
You can get rid of the unaligned access errors in several ways.

1) Add "#pragama function (memcpy)" to your source code prior to the
memcpy() call.
    This will cause the compiler to generate a call to the memcpy()
function.

2) Add a cast with __unaligned to your memcpy call.  For example, if
structure is of type foo:

        memcpy( (__unaligned struct foo *)&structure->lrc, &lrc, 1) ;

3) Add "-nointrinsics" to your cc command line.  This will work but will
degrade performance
   for all calls that could be compiler intrinsics.

I'd recommend using the __unaligned cast.

Joe Mario
Compaq Tru64 Development Environment




