> I have two questions relating to the passwd file on Digital Unix 4.0E.  I have
> two alpha workstations with this OS.  One is configured with enhanced security
> and I can add a user OK with the command line adduser, but the system disables
> the account until I go in with the GUI tool and enable it.  This is a little
> bit of a pain since the box is about 20 miles away.  Does anyone know of a
> method to enable an account with a command line?

I haven't tried it, but I've been told to use edauth... From looking at
the man page, it's fairly cryptic.

Personally, I really wish there were some good command-line enhanced
security account tools. Either that or make the regular adduser, passwd,
etc fully enhanced security aware... But if wishes were horses...

If I wanted to be stuck to a GUI account manager, I'd run NT... :)

> On the second box, I am using base security.  If I use adduser, whenever it
> calls useradd, I get an error message that some other process has the passwd
> or group file locked (even if I am in single user mode).  I can use vipw and
> add the user and manually create the directory, etc.  However, the user keeps
> getting the error message that there is no directory until I put the user into
> the system group in /etc/group.  Does anyone have a clue where I could start
> looking for this culprit?

This one is easy:
(http://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/dec-faq/Digital-UNIX):

	M18. Why does dxaccounts tells me that the password and 
	group files are locked?

	If a dxaccounts process exits ungracefully (e.g. if a 
	kill -9 is used) then it sometimes leaves a file in 
	/etc called .AM_is_running. Deleting this file will
	solve the problem.
                                        [Ian Lloyd]

My understanding is this is something (finally) fixed in Tru64 (5.0??).
