Hi Brandon,

I am not in the neighbourhood of an Alpha, so I have to give you a few
hints from my memory, which has no parity check enabled ;-)

I am surprised you have nrmt10h, I don't expect you to have at least
11 tape units, right? Did this ever work? Did you install fresh
hardware (SCSI controller)?

Best thing to do:
1. Inspect the syslog for the boot messages, to find the name of the
tape device. Search for something like  "tz" or "TZ".
2. If you find nothing, is it possible that you did not build a new
kernel after installing hardware? If you find nothing, shutdown your
system, and from the boot prompt, type "show device", it should show
the tape device. If not, there's something wrong with your hardware.

If the boot messages report the tape unit, do:
3. cd /dev
4. rm *rmt*   # remove all links to tape units
5. ./MAKEDEV tz14 (for example, if tz14 was the thing you found in the
boot messages. In general, the number will be 8 (second SCSI
controller) plus the SCSI-id of the tape unit (in my example 6).

Hope this helps a bit!

Regards,
Eric

